Falsifies `system_tests::user::tests::a_reply_carries_a_capability_the_caller_can_use`.

Architecture: aarch64, riscv64, x86_64

The defect is `REPLY_CAPABILITY` carrying a capability without the delegation check: `ipc_reply_capability`
files the server's capability as it stands instead of passing it through `Delegation::derive`, so
a capability held without `GRANT` reaches the caller. That is the one rule that makes a reply a
delegation rather than a copy anyone may make.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(server & 0b0001, 0b0001, "a capability held without GRANT was
carried by a reply")`. Confirmed red on 2026-10-07 (UTC) at `system_tests/src/user/tests.rs:3437`
on aarch64, with `script/test --arch aarch64 --test a_reply_carries_a_capability`. The hunk is
portable code, so one patch serves every leg; riscv64 and x86_64 were not replayed by hand. Re-cut the same day, unchanged in substance, after
`ipc_reply_capability` learned to report a copy that did not land; the re-cut hunk was not replayed
again.

diff --git a/kernel/src/sched.rs b/kernel/src/sched.rs
index ca3d9f108..1a90d41a2 100644
--- a/kernel/src/sched.rs
+++ b/kernel/src/sched.rs
@@ -4391,11 +4391,11 @@ pub fn ipc_reply_capability(caller: ThreadId, msg: [u64; 2], slot: u64) -> Result<
             .ok_or(abi::Error::NoSuchSlot)?;
         let table = ours.lock();
         let src = table.get(slot).map_err(|_| abi::Error::NoSuchSlot)?;
-        Delegation {
+        let _ = Delegation {
             slot,
             rights: src.rights,
-        }
-        .derive(&table)?
+        };
+        src
     };
     let delivered;
     {
